+# Receipts not sending
+
+For the volunteer on call when members report no receipt emails. Drains the backlog and gets receipts flowing again;
+while it is down, captured pantry orders sit unreceipted and members ask why. Written after the second time it
+happened, in September 2026, when the volunteer on call did not know what to do and Tomás had to be woken up. Owner:
+the payments volunteers.
+
+## Before you begin
+
+- SSH access to the worker host, to run `systemctl` and `journalctl`.
+- `psql` access to the `larder` database.
+
+## Steps
+
+1. Check the worker: `systemctl status larder-worker`.
+
+   Expected: `active (running)`. If it says `inactive (dead)`, that is the cause: the deploy script only restarts the
+   web app, not the worker, so a deploy can leave it dead.
+2. Count the backlog: `psql larder -c "select status, count(*) from procrastinate_jobs group by status"`.
+
+   Expected: a number of `todo` jobs, roughly one per receipt that hasn't gone out. A handful of `failed` jobs is
+   normal and not part of this incident: they are bounced addresses the member needs to fix, not something the worker
+   can retry.
+3. Start the worker: `sudo systemctl start larder-worker`.
+
+   Expected: `systemctl status larder-worker` now says `active (running)`.
+4. Re-run the count query from step 2 every minute or so.
+
+   Expected: `todo` drains to 0, at roughly 40 a minute. `failed` does not grow past what it was in step 2.
+
+## If it fails
+
+If the worker goes back to `inactive (dead)` right after step 3, collect `journalctl -u larder-worker -n 50` and ping
+Tomás with it.
